WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.817
Imagine a cleaner paid so little that rent and food are hard to afford. The government can pass a law saying no one may be paid less than a set amount. That law is a minimum wage.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:26.869
Why have one? To raise the incomes and living standards of the lowest paid, to narrow the gap between rich and poor, and to reduce the need for government benefits.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:40.365
There is a risk. If it is set above the equilibrium wage, firms want fewer workers, Q d, while more people want to work, Q s. The gap is a surplus of labour: unemployment.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:57.362
Step one, define it. Step two, use the case's numbers. For example, a rise from five hundred to five hundred and fifty euros is fifty euros. Fifty divided by five hundred, times one hundred, is ten per cent.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:13.755
Step three, benefit chains. Workers get more income and better living standards. They spend more, so firms' revenue rises. Workers may be more motivated, and the government may collect more tax.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:25.724
Step four, cost chains. Firms' wage bills rise. They may hire fewer workers, cut hours, raise prices or accept lower profits.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:37.814
Step five, judge. The effect depends on the size of the rise, whether productivity rises, price elasticity of demand, the share of workers paid it, and the short or long run.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:57.567
Here is a real question from June 2025. Latvia raised its minimum wage from six hundred and twenty to seven hundred euros a month. Sixteen per cent of people were paid it. Evaluate whether this would benefit everyone. Twelve marks.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:09.116
Define it, then use the data. Seven hundred minus six hundred and twenty is eighty euros. Eighty divided by six hundred and twenty, times one hundred, is about twelve point nine per cent.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:24.880
Benefits to workers. Eighty euros more a month buys more food, housing and essentials, so living standards rise. Feeling better rewarded, they may work harder, raising productivity.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:40.327
Benefits to firms and government. Low-paid workers spend most of any extra income, so shops in Latvia earn more revenue. Higher incomes and spending also bring more tax for public services.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:56.428
But not everyone gains. Wage costs rise, and some firms hire fewer workers or cut hours. If the wage is pushed further above equilibrium, unemployment can rise, and those who lose jobs are worse off.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:18.964
Judge. Many benefit, but not everyone. Only sixteen per cent are paid the minimum, so for many firms the extra cost is small, and big job losses are less likely. In the short run some small firms may lose out. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:29.817
A common mistake is saying a minimum wage always causes unemployment. It depends on the size of the rise and on productivity.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:39.049
Another is saying it helps firms without saying how. Explain the link through spending or motivation.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:58.633
Now you try. An example country raises its minimum wage from eight dollars to eight dollars sixty an hour. Calculate the percentage rise. Then give one benefit for workers and one cost for firms. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:07.977
The rise is sixty cents. Sixty cents divided by eight dollars, times one hundred, is seven point five per cent.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:18.775
Benefit: workers earn more each hour, so living standards rise. Cost: firms' wage bills rise, so they may hire fewer workers.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:29.455
Remember: define it and use the data, set benefits against costs, then judge what it depends on. Now try the practice questions.
